    {
      "id": "http://denersteinunleashed.blogspot.com/2026/09/verity-not-enough-trashy-fun.html",
      "url": "http://denersteinunleashed.blogspot.com/2026/09/verity-not-enough-trashy-fun.html",
      "title": "[Kagi Small Web] 'Verity': Not enough trashy fun",
      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=kagi.com&amp;sz=128\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p style=\"line-height: normal; margin: 0px;\"><span style=\"font-family: Helvetica Neue; font-size: 11px; font-style: normal;\">&nbsp;</span><span><span style=\"font-family: inherit; font-size: 11px; font-style: normal;\">&nbsp;\\</span></span></p><p style=\"line-height: normal; margin: 0px;\"><span><span style=\"font-size: large;\"><span style=\"font-family: inherit;\"><br /></span></span></span></p><p style=\"line-height: normal; margin: 0px;\"><span><span style=\"font-size: large;\"><span style=\"font-family: inherit;\"><br /></span></span></span></p><p style=\"line-height: normal; margin: 0px;\"></p><div class=\"separator\" style=\"clear: both; text-align: center;\"><a href=\"https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/a/AVvXsEijDOxE4jOVlCbOSoNcojiYXA3wqoAgFZD1a-e57-31HHRprw4IfFqNOcWB-a5A6Vbq6cWiy0SjCkiA-auIxUagZecwrbmIvWWVUDppELBh36j0c9jK3E3EakDy3DAJtUfN0xMavgwjtjfmTHvQPmdpsQ0MiwauidJayYidUkNBUfsgNydyhQxmkRtGpF4\" style=\"clear: right; float: right; margin-bottom: 1em; margin-left: 1em;\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"180\" src=\"https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/a/AVvXsEijDOxE4jOVlCbOSoNcojiYXA3wqoAgFZD1a-e57-31HHRprw4IfFqNOcWB-a5A6Vbq6cWiy0SjCkiA-auIxUagZecwrbmIvWWVUDppELBh36j0c9jK3E3EakDy3DAJtUfN0xMavgwjtjfmTHvQPmdpsQ0MiwauidJayYidUkNBUfsgNydyhQxmkRtGpF4\" width=\"320\" /></a></div><span><span style=\"font-size: large;\"><span style=\"font-family: inherit;\"><br />There’s nothing wrong with good, trashy fun. That’s what I hoped to get from </span><b style=\"font-family: inherit;\"><i>Verity,</i></b><span style=\"font-family: inherit;\"> the fourth big-screen adaptation of a novel by the widely read&nbsp;</span><b style=\"font-family: inherit;\">Colleen Hoover</b><span style=\"font-family: inherit;\">. After some initial optimism, my hopes steadily dissolved. A cast composed of </span><b style=\"font-family: inherit;\">Dakota Johnson</b><span style=\"font-family: inherit;\">, </span><b style=\"font-family: inherit;\">Anne Hathaway</b><span style=\"font-family: inherit;\">, and </span><b style=\"font-family: inherit;\">Josh Hartnett </b><span style=\"font-family: inherit;\">seemed ready to deliver a story that makes room for sexual attraction, deception and blurred identity lines. In her fifth </span>movie<span style=\"font-family: inherit;\">&nbsp;this year, Hathaway portrays Verity Crawford, a popular author whose stories &nbsp;are told from the villain’s point of view. Johnson plays a struggling novelist who’s chosen to finish one of Verity’s books after an automobile accident leaves the author unable to walk or talk. Hartnett plays Verity’s husband, Jeremy, a charming enough fellow who develops a relationship with Johnson’s Lowen after she moves into the couple’s sleek Vermont home. Research, you know. We also meet Crew (</span><b style=\"font-family: inherit;\">Brady Wagner)</b><span style=\"font-family: inherit;\">, the eight-year-old surviving Crawford twin. His sister Harper (</span><b style=\"font-family: inherit;\">Sienna Kulovitz</b><span style=\"font-family: inherit;\">) drowned in a canoeing accident. Cue the guilt and add a </span>secret<span style=\"font-family: inherit;\">&nbsp;</span>autobiography<span style=\"font-family: inherit;\">&nbsp;Verity had been writing. Director <b>Michael</b>&nbsp;</span><b style=\"font-family: inherit;\">Showalter</b><span style=\"font-family: inherit;\"> offers glossy images and tries to </span>add<span style=\"font-family: inherit;\">&nbsp;layers of suspense. Immobile through much of the movie, Hathaway still manages to register. So does Hartnett, but Johnson’s performance tends toward dullness. Oh well, the initial glamor loses its luster, and a plethora of melodramatic third-act plot twists wreck what's left of the movie's waning&nbsp;</span>plausibility.&nbsp;<span style=\"font-family: inherit;\">As for samples of Verity’s writing ... oh, hell, let's just move on.</span></span></span><p></p><p style=\"line-height: normal; margin: 0px;\"><span><span style=\"font-size: large;\"><span style=\"font-family: inherit;\"><br /></span></span></span></p><br />",
