      "title": "[Trip Reports] Well, the bugs weren't bad",
      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=backcountrypost.com&amp;sz=128\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=backcountrypost.com&amp;sz=128\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=backcountrypost.com&amp;sz=128\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=backcountrypost.com&amp;sz=128\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=backcountrypost.com&amp;sz=128\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<div class=\"bbWrapper\">I had made plans to hike up the Lamar River, a little ways, just to see what it looked like. Last year, I had gotten to the edge of the Thorofare, and saw a NEW VIEW in Yellowstone.  I thought that maybe, around the corner from where everybody drives, there might be another new view, so when the time to sign up for trips came along, I got a couple of nights a little ways up the Lamar River Trail. Not far, mind you, but maybe enough to see something new.  When the time came closer, I found...<br />\n<br />\n<a class=\"link link--internal\" href=\"https://backcountrypost.com/threads/well-the-bugs-werent-bad.12732/\">Read more</a></div>",
